Added `cancel(String paymentId)` to `src/main/java/mn/pay/PaymentService.java:35`:

```java
@Transactional
public Payment cancel(String paymentId) {
    Payment p = repository.findById(paymentId)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalArgumentException("unknown payment: " + paymentId));
    if (!"PENDING".equals(p.getStatus())) {
        throw new IllegalStateException(
                "only a PENDING payment can be cancelled, was " + p.getStatus());
    }
    p.setStatus("CANCELLED");
    return repository.save(p);
}
```

It follows the same shape as `create`: `@Transactional` so the status read and the write can't interleave with a concurrent cancel, matching the reasoning already documented on the idempotency check. Unknown id gives `IllegalArgumentException` (consistent with `PaymentValidator`'s argument failures); a non-PENDING status gives `IllegalStateException`, since that's a state problem rather than a bad argument.

Also added three tests to `PaymentServiceTest` covering the happy path, a non-PENDING payment (asserts the status is left untouched and nothing is saved), and an unknown id. `mvn -o test` passes.

Two things I deliberately left out, since you asked only for the service method — say the word if you want either:
- No `cancelPayment` mutation in `payment.graphqls` / `PaymentController`, so it isn't reachable over the API yet.
- No DB-level guard. The status check is transactional, but under `READ_COMMITTED` (Postgres/MySQL default) two concurrent cancels can both read PENDING and both write; a `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` or a status-conditional update would close that. Not a correctness issue for cancel specifically — the second write is idempotent — but it would matter if a PENDING→SETTLED transition is added later.
